    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d651">

                <pb facs="0812" n="800" sameAs="frus:frus1943v03/pg800"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00114 Pacific War/138: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Standley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Kuibyshev</hi> (<hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi>)</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-02-06T20:00:00+03:00">February 6, 1943—8 p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 7—3:50 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>118. Department’s 41, January 18. I outlined to Vyshinski<anchor n="70"
                        xml:id="d651-f1"/> on February 5 the considerations set forth in the
                    Department’s telegram and left with him an <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi> on the question. Vyshinski promised to bring the proposal
                    immediately to the attention of his Government. I requested an expeditious
                    reply, stressing the desperate situation of the United Nations nationals in
                    Japanese custody.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Standley</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d651-f1" n="70" xml:id="d651-f1-content">
                <p>Andrey Yanuaryevich Vyshinsky, First Assistant People’s Commissar for Foreign
                    Affairs of the Soviet Union.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
